# Soil Strength Predictor using Machine Learning

## Project Overview

This project presents a Machine Learning-based Soil Strength Prediction System capable of estimating two important geotechnical engineering parameters:

1. California Bearing Ratio (CBR)
2. Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS)

The application utilizes trained machine learning models to predict soil strength characteristics from user-provided input parameters. The system is designed to assist civil engineers, geotechnical engineers, researchers, and students in obtaining quick and reliable estimates of soil properties without extensive laboratory testing.

## Objectives

- Develop accurate machine learning models for predicting CBR and UCS values.
- Reduce the time and cost associated with conventional laboratory testing.
- Provide a user-friendly graphical interface for easy prediction.
- Demonstrate the application of Artificial Intelligence in geotechnical engineering.

## Methodology

The project involves the following steps:

1. Collection and preprocessing of soil datasets.
2. Data cleaning, normalization, and feature scaling.
3. Training multiple machine learning algorithms.
4. Selection of the best-performing models based on evaluation metrics.
5. Saving trained models and scaler objects using Pickle (.pkl) files.
6. Development of a Graphical User Interface (GUI) for real-time predictions.

## Project Features

- Predicts California Bearing Ratio (CBR).
- Predicts Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS).
- Uses pre-trained machine learning models.
- Interactive GUI for user input and result visualization.
- Fast and efficient prediction process.
- Easy deployment and reproducibility.

